= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under the convention $e^{i(kx-\omega t)}$, a mode's amplitude grows as $e^{\omega_i t}$. The <linear stability analysis> of real-<wavenumber> disturbances is therefore summarized by
$$
\boxed{\omega_{i,\max}<0:\ \text{strictly stable},\qquad
\omega_{i,\max}>0:\ \text{linearly unstable}.}
$$
At $\omega_{i,\max}=0$ there is marginal or neutral <hydrodynamic stability>: no <normal mode> grows exponentially, but some may fail to decay. If “linearly stable” includes neutral modes, its condition is $\omega_{i,\max}\leq0$. These modal statements do not rule out <transient growth from non-normal modes> or algebraic growth.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The fixed observer is the ray $V=0$. The physically selected zero-<group velocity> saddle defines the <absolute wavenumber> $k_0$ and <absolute frequency> $\omega_0$:
$$
\boxed{\omega'(k_0)=0,\qquad D(k_0,\omega_0)=0,\qquad\omega_0=\omega(k_0).}
$$
For a smooth frequency branch with $D_\omega\ne0$, the saddle condition is equivalently $D_k(k_0,\omega_0)=0$. The <absolute growth rate> is
$$
\boxed{\sigma(0)=\operatorname{Im}\omega_0.}
$$
Here $k_0$ and $\omega_0$ can be complex. A physical <saddle point> is selected by the localized impulse response and deformation of its <Fourier transform> contour; an arbitrary algebraic stationary point need not determine <absolute hydrodynamic instability>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a temporally unstable base flow, the relevant <saddle growth rate along a ray> distinguishes two cases in the chosen observation frame:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\omega_{i,\max}>0,\quad\sigma(0)<0&:\ \text{convective hydrodynamic instability},\\
\sigma(0)>0&:\ \text{absolute hydrodynamic instability}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
A <convective hydrodynamic instability> amplifies a travelling <wave packet> but lets a localized disturbance decay at any fixed point. An <absolute hydrodynamic instability> grows at a fixed point. The separating case $\sigma(0)=0$ is marginal in exponential rate and can have an algebraic prefactor. In a frame moving with $V$, replace $\sigma(0)$ by $\sigma(V)=\operatorname{Im}(\omega_\star-Vk_\star)$: this distinction depends on the observation frame.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the <linear complex Ginzburg-Landau equation>, take $U,c_d,\mu$ real. **$U$ gives <advection>**, carrying the envelope downstream at speed $U$. **$c_d$ gives <wave dispersion>**: the imaginary part of the second-derivative coefficient changes wave phase without directly dissipating a real-<wavenumber> <Fourier mode>. **$\mu$ gives local linear production or damping**: a spatially uniform perturbation grows for $\mu>0$ and decays for $\mu<0$.

The real part of the diffusion coefficient is the scaled value one. It smooths the envelope and damps a real-<wavenumber> <Fourier mode> at rate $k^2$. Thus the <linear complex Ginzburg-Landau equation> models competing <advection>, smoothing through a <diffusion equation>, <wave dispersion> and local growth, rather than nonlinear saturation.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a <normal mode>, $\partial_t\mapsto-i\omega$, $\partial_x\mapsto ik$, and $\partial_x^2\mapsto-k^2$. Substitution into the <linear complex Ginzburg-Landau equation> gives
$$
-i\omega+ikU-\mu+(1+ic_d)k^2=0.
$$
Therefore **the dispersion relation is**
$$
\boxed{D(k,\omega)=-i\omega+ikU-\mu+(1+ic_d)k^2=0,\qquad
\omega=Uk+(c_d-i)k^2+i\mu.}
$$
For real $k$, its <temporal growth rate> is $\omega_i=\mu-k^2$, with unique maximum $\mu$ at $k=0$. Its real <angular frequency> is $Uk+c_dk^2$, displaying the separate <advection> and <wave dispersion> terms.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <dispersion relation> gives the zero-<group velocity> saddle
$$
k_0=-\frac{U}{2(c_d-i)},\qquad
\omega_0=i\mu-\frac{U^2}{4(c_d-i)}
=-\frac{U^2c_d}{4(1+c_d^2)}+i\left[\mu-\frac{U^2}{4(1+c_d^2)}\right].
$$
Thus the <absolute growth rate> is $\sigma(0)=\mu-U^2/[4(1+c_d^2)]$. More generally the <saddle growth rate along a ray> is
$$
\sigma(V)=\mu-\frac{(U-V)^2}{4(1+c_d^2)},
$$
so a frame travelling at $V=U$ sees the maximal rate $\mu$.

For $U>0$, **the three open regions are**
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mu<0&:\ \text{linear stability},\\
0<\mu<\frac{U^2}{4(1+c_d^2)}&:\ \text{convective hydrodynamic instability},\\
\mu>\frac{U^2}{4(1+c_d^2)}&:\ \text{absolute hydrodynamic instability}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The line $\mu=0$ is the temporal marginal boundary; the parabola $\mu=U^2/[4(1+c_d^2)]$ is the convective-to-absolute boundary. The <Green function of the linear complex Ginzburg-Landau equation> has the prefactor $t^{-1/2}$, so zero exponential rate on the latter boundary still allows algebraic decay of the impulse response. This is the <stability diagram of the linear complex Ginzburg-Landau equation>.

